---
// SEO Educational Copy: Complete Guide to Measuring Angles
---

<section id="guide" class="mt-16 pt-12 border-t border-zinc-800 text-zinc-300">
  <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto space-y-12">
    <!-- Main Headline -->
    <div>
      <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-extrabold text-zinc-100 tracking-tight">
        How to Measure Angles with an Online Protractor
      </h2>
      <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-zinc-400 leading-relaxed">
        A protractor measures the geometric angle formed between two intersecting lines or rays sharing a common vertex. Whether you are solving trigonometry homework, verifying carpentry cut angles, or analyzing CAD drawings, this guide explains angle classifications, conversion formulas, and measurement techniques.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Quick Step Guide -->
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3 gap-4">
      <div class="bg-zinc-900 border border-zinc-800 p-5 rounded-xl space-y-2">
        <div class="w-8 h-8 rounded-lg bg-sky-500/10 text-sky-400 font-bold flex items-center justify-center text-sm border border-sky-500/30">1</div>
        <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-zinc-100">Align the Vertex</h3>
        <p class="text-xs text-zinc-400 leading-relaxed">
          Drag the blue central origin handle directly onto the intersection point of the two rays you need to measure.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="bg-zinc-900 border border-zinc-800 p-5 rounded-xl space-y-2">
        <div class="w-8 h-8 rounded-lg bg-orange-500/10 text-orange-400 font-bold flex items-center justify-center text-sm border border-orange-500/30">2</div>
        <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-zinc-100">Set the Baseline</h3>
        <p class="text-xs text-zinc-400 leading-relaxed">
          Position the orange handle (Ray A) flat against the base edge or reference horizon of your measured shape.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="bg-zinc-900 border border-zinc-800 p-5 rounded-xl space-y-2">
        <div class="w-8 h-8 rounded-lg bg-emerald-500/10 text-emerald-400 font-bold flex items-center justify-center text-sm border border-emerald-500/30">3</div>
        <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-zinc-100">Read the Angle</h3>
        <p class="text-xs text-zinc-400 leading-relaxed">
          Drag the cyan handle (Ray B) along the second line. The tool calculates degrees, radians, and slope in real time.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Comprehensive Angle Types Breakdown -->
    <div class="space-y-6">
      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-zinc-100">Angle Classifications & Degree Ranges</h3>
      <p class="text-xs text-zinc-400">
        In Euclidean planar geometry, angles are classified into six distinct categories based on their rotational magnitude relative to a straight line (180°) and a full circle (360°).
      </p>

      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3 gap-4">
        <!-- Acute -->
        <div class="bg-zinc-900/60 border border-zinc-800/80 p-4 rounded-xl space-y-2">
          <div class="flex items-center justify-between">
            <span class="font-semibold text-sm text-zinc-100">Acute Angle</span>
            <span class="text-xs font-mono text-sky-400">&gt; 0° and &lt; 90°</span>
          </div>
          <p class="text-xs text-zinc-400 leading-relaxed">
            Smaller than a right angle. Found in triangular roof trusses, scissors, and equilateral triangles (where all three angles equal 60°).
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Right -->
        <div class="bg-zinc-900/60 border border-zinc-800/80 p-4 rounded-xl space-y-2">
          <div class="flex items-center justify-between">
            <span class="font-semibold text-sm text-zinc-100">Right Angle</span>
            <span class="text-xs font-mono text-emerald-400">Exactly 90°</span>
          </div>
          <p class="text-xs text-zinc-400 leading-relaxed">
            Represents perpendicular lines, or a quarter-turn. Walls, door frames, squares, and rectangles rely on perpendicular 90° corners.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Obtuse -->
        <div class="bg-zinc-900/60 border border-zinc-800/80 p-4 rounded-xl space-y-2">
          <div class="flex items-center justify-between">
            <span class="font-semibold text-sm text-zinc-100">Obtuse Angle</span>
            <span class="text-xs font-mono text-amber-400">&gt; 90° and &lt; 180°</span>
          </div>
          <p class="text-xs text-zinc-400 leading-relaxed">
            Wider than a right angle but smaller than a straight line. Common in stop signs (octagons have 135° interior angles) and reclined chairs.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Straight -->
        <div class="bg-zinc-900/60 border border-zinc-800/80 p-4 rounded-xl space-y-2">
          <div class="flex items-center justify-between">
            <span class="font-semibold text-sm text-zinc-100">Straight Angle</span>
            <span class="text-xs font-mono text-purple-400">Exactly 180°</span>
          </div>
          <p class="text-xs text-zinc-400 leading-relaxed">
            Forms a completely flat horizontal or vertical line, equivalent to a half-turn or π radians. Two right angles summed equal 180°.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Reflex -->
        <div class="bg-zinc-900/60 border border-zinc-800/80 p-4 rounded-xl space-y-2">
          <div class="flex items-center justify-between">
            <span class="font-semibold text-sm text-zinc-100">Reflex Angle</span>
            <span class="text-xs font-mono text-rose-400">&gt; 180° and &lt; 360°</span>
          </div>
          <p class="text-xs text-zinc-400 leading-relaxed">
            Greater than a straight line but less than a full rotation. When a clock shows 8:00, the outer arc between the hands forms a reflex angle of 240°.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Complete / Full -->
        <div class="bg-zinc-900/60 border border-zinc-800/80 p-4 rounded-xl space-y-2">
          <div class="flex items-center justify-between">
            <span class="font-semibold text-sm text-zinc-100">Full Angle (Perigon)</span>
            <span class="text-xs font-mono text-zinc-300">Exactly 360°</span>
          </div>
          <p class="text-xs text-zinc-400 leading-relaxed">
            A full 360-degree rotation where both arms overlap completely, equivalent to 2π radians or a complete circle.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Mathematical Formulas Reference Table -->
    <div class="space-y-4">
      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-zinc-100">Conversion Formulas & Mathematical Relationships</h3>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-zinc-800">
        <table class="w-full text-left text-xs">
          <thead class="bg-zinc-900 text-zinc-300 border-b border-zinc-800">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">Measurement</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">Formula</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">Example (θ = 45°)</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-zinc-800 text-zinc-400">
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium text-zinc-200">Radians to Degrees</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 font-mono">Degrees = Radians × (180 / π)</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 font-mono">π/4 × (180/π) = 45°</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium text-zinc-200">Degrees to Radians</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 font-mono">Radians = Degrees × (π / 180)</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 font-mono">45 × (π/180) = 0.7854 rad</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium text-zinc-200">Supplementary Angle</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 font-mono">Supplementary = 180° - θ</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 font-mono">180° - 45° = 135°</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium text-zinc-200">Complementary Angle</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 font-mono">Complementary = 90° - θ</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 font-mono">90° - 45° = 45°</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium text-zinc-200">Slope / Gradient (%)</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 font-mono">Slope = tan(θ) × 100</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 font-mono">tan(45°) × 100 = 100%</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Practical Real-World Use Cases -->
    <div class="space-y-4">
      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-zinc-100">Common Applications for Digital Protractors</h3>
      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4 text-xs">
        <div class="bg-zinc-900/40 border border-zinc-800 p-4 rounded-xl space-y-2">
          <h4 class="font-semibold text-zinc-200">Carpentry & Miter Cuts</h4>
          <p class="text-zinc-400 leading-relaxed">
            Trim carpenters use corner angle readings to split joint angles on miter saws. If two baseboard walls form an 88° acute angle, each miter cut must be set to precisely 44°.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-zinc-900/40 border border-zinc-800 p-4 rounded-xl space-y-2">
          <h4 class="font-semibold text-zinc-200">Roof Pitch & Staircase Grade</h4>
          <p class="text-zinc-400 leading-relaxed">
            By taking a side photo of a staircase or roof rafter and uploading it into the camera tool, contractors calculate slope percentages and pitch ratios without carrying heavy manual levels.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-zinc-900/40 border border-zinc-800 p-4 rounded-xl space-y-2">
          <h4 class="font-semibold text-zinc-200">Classroom Geometry Homework</h4>
          <p class="text-zinc-400 leading-relaxed">
            Students take a photo of textbook diagrams or polygon assignments, overlay the transparent protractor, and verify triangle interior angles sum to 180°.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-zinc-900/40 border border-zinc-800 p-4 rounded-xl space-y-2">
          <h4 class="font-semibold text-zinc-200">Graphic Design & UI Layouts</h4>
          <p class="text-zinc-400 leading-relaxed">
            Designers paste screenshots (<kbd class="px-1 py-0.5 bg-zinc-800 border border-zinc-700 rounded text-zinc-300">Ctrl+V</kbd>) to confirm isometric angles (typically 30°), diagonal brand slashes, or icon rotations.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
